The juice carton. Also the company that makes it.

A shelf-stable carton is about 70% paperboard, 25% [[Polyethylene]], and 5% [[Aluminum]] foil, pressed into one sheet. There is no wax. What feels like wax is the plastic.

Each layer is there for a reason:

<<<
Every layer has a specific job to do: the paperboard gives the package its rigidity and strength, the polymers keep moisture out, and the thin aluminium barrier protects against light and oxygen.
<<< [[Tetra Pak|https://www.tetrapak.com/en-ie/solutions/packaging/packaging-material]]

So the carton works very well, right up until it is empty.

!! Is it recyclable?

Tetra Pak says so. 63% of US households can put one in the bin. Only about 20% of cartons actually get recycled, by the carton industry's own count.

And "recycled" mostly means the paper. A mill soaks the carton until the paper fiber comes loose. The plastic and aluminum come out still stuck to each other. That leftover is called [[PolyAl]]. It gets pressed into something lesser ([[Downcycling]]), burned for energy, or landfilled.

The other route skips the soaking. The whole carton is shredded and pressed into roof board.

So the paper half gets a second life. The hybrid half stays a hybrid.

See [[Monstrous Hybrid]], [[Juice Pouch]], [[Recyclable, but really?]]

